[THOUGHT] The patient is uncertain about occupational exposures, travel, or zoonotic risks — this lack of recall doesn’t rule out relevant exposures, but it reduces suspicion for classic infectious triggers (e.g., Lyme, brucellosis). Given the isolated, progressive monoarticular knee swelling over two months with NSAID resistance, the next highest-yield step is a targeted physical examination — specifically to assess objective signs that discriminate among diagnostic possibilities:  
- Warmth and fluctuance suggest inflammatory/infected effusion;  
- Boggy vs. firm swelling may hint at PVNS or tumor;  
- Range of motion limitation, crepitus, or mechanical symptoms (locking, catching) point to structural or intra-articular pathology;  
- Skin changes (erythema, violaceous hue, nodules) could indicate malignancy or chronic inflammation.  

I must request a focused physical exam — atomic and specific — starting with inspection and palpation of the left knee.

[TEST] Perform targeted physical examination of the left knee: inspect for erythema, swelling distribution, skin changes (e.g., violaceous discoloration, nodules), and palpate for warmth, tenderness (location: medial/lateral joint line, patella, suprapatellar pouch), and fluid (ballottement, fluid wave).

